<p>New reporting lays out how Jeffrey Epstein kept seeking — and often maintaining — access to prominent institutions and individuals after his sex-crime conviction. The stories describe a familiar playbook: donations, social ties and persistence that helped him stay in elite social, academic and media circles. One set of articles looks at Epstein’s support for a Harvard-linked nonprofit connected to student groups and an annual gala. Another focuses on emails showing Nautilus founder and publisher John Steele reaching out to Epstein while looking for financial backing for the magazine.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/I3wGxNhmoIFsaap-ZyWNIcMgyNL1vO17pJiLzKxDuFl2KJw1-zCxmiladHvekTg_ZOE4862sCH2C_RC6Yfe83Nj10Srfz-gCqsiFYPeG2HM8BJzb-s197UAHiGFe4TxOnCAil4RDDYPw6rqZW69dp_wGZ-MhgiYmEpgdyr2dI1y7hpV0' alt='Jeffrey Epstein, in &#x27;Cosmopolitan&#x27; magazine, July 1980.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Harvard-linked giving: The reporting says Epstein donated at least US$50,000 a year to the Hasty Pudding Institute of 1770 for years after his conviction, earning “Guardian” status reserved for top contributors.</li><li>Gala access: The reports say Epstein was able for years to fill a table with “girls” at the group’s annual gala — an event described as drawing celebrities, billionaires and cultural figures.</li><li>Email outreach: In a Dec. 14, 2017 email, Steele opened by nodding to Epstein’s reputation and linked to an Undark story about Nautilus freelancers seeking US$50,000 in unpaid commissions.</li><li>Network mechanics: Le Monde traces Epstein’s rise from a modest background and says he used manipulation and scheming to build a global network — one that kept parts of the elite associating with him even after his conviction.</li></ul><blockquote>“Jeffrey, As someone who is no stranger to controversy… I thought you might appreciate how I spent my day yesterday.” - John Steele</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>John Steele (Nautilus founder): Semafor reported emails showing Steele sought to build a relationship with Epstein in 2017, referencing Epstein’s notoriety while discussing pressures on Nautilus. (<a href='https://www.semafor.com/article/02/15/2026/science-magazine-founder-courted-epstein-for-cash-emails-show'>Semafor</a>)</li><li>Le Monde analysis: Le Monde argues Epstein deliberately cultivated relationships across politics, business and media, maintaining ties with influential people even after his 2008 conviction. (<a href='https://www.lemonde.fr/en/international/article/2026/02/15/how-jeffrey-epstein-built-the-global-network-that-protected-him_6750514_4.html'>Le Monde</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.semafor.com/article/02/15/2026/science-magazine-founder-courted-epstein-for-cash-emails-show'>Science magazine founder courted Epstein for cash, emails show</a> - semafor.com</li><li><a href='https://www.lemonde.fr/en/international/article/2026/02/15/how-jeffrey-epstein-built-the-global-network-that-protected-him_6750514_4.html'>How Jeffrey Epstein built the global network that protected him</a> - lemonde.fr</li><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/united-states/hello-girls-epstein-donated-to-harvard-student-group-for-years-after-sex-conviction'>‘Hello girls!’: Epstein donated to Harvard student group for years after sex conviction</a> - straitstimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/united-states-canada/article/3343658/hello-girls-epstein-was-hasty-pudding-donor-years-after-sex-conviction'>Epstein donated to Harvard student group for years after sex conviction</a> - scmp.com</li></ul>